// --- the compile-time guard's faults (docs/design/windows-jit.md §4) --------------------------------------------------------
// Linux catches a bad access in a compile-time call with a SIGSEGV handler that jumps back to the guard with 4. Call 4004 arms
// the same for the calling thread on Windows: a vectored exception handler, ahead of the crash report's, sends an access
// violation (0xC0000005) or a stack overflow (0xC00000FD) to the target, the slice's fault handler, as the signal would: with
// (11, info), the faulting address at info + 16 where siginfo's si_addr is. Only a fault in JIT'd code (no loaded image holds
// the program counter) or in the program's own image is taken: an exception a system DLL raises is often one it handles
// itself, and goes on to the next handler. The thread reserves 64 KiB for the handler first (SetThreadStackGuarantee), so it
// runs even on a stack that overflowed. A fault is taken once: the target jumps back, and the guard arms the next call again.

